# Mobile Architecture Overview

Architecture defines where a responsibility belongs and which dependencies are allowed. Use this section to design features that can be understood, tested, and maintained across Android, iOS, and React Native.

**React Native with TypeScript and Redux Toolkit is the strongly preferred starting point for new applications.** Native Android and iOS remain supported where requirements justify them. Existing applications keep their established stack unless a migration is explicitly agreed.

These principles apply across the supported stacks. Their concrete UI, state, concurrency, persistence, and dependency-injection mechanisms belong in Platforms & SDKs.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep architecture decisions, internal service details, source, and diagnostic evidence within approved systems. Use synthetic examples and never include credentials or sensitive user data in diagrams or code.

## 1. What this section helps you decide

Use these pages when planning or reviewing a feature:

| Question | Architectural decision |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Where does this logic belong?** | Separate presentation, business rules, and concrete data access. |
| **Who owns this state and work?** | Define lifecycle, cancellation, account scope, and stale-result handling. |
| **What happens when a dependency fails?** | Define timeouts, failure classification, safe retries, circuit breaking, and permitted fallback. |
| **How can behavior change safely?** | Define feature defaults, refresh behavior, rollout, and kill-switch limitations. |
| **How is a change integrated?** | Follow repository branching, review, and required-check conventions. |

Workstation installation belongs in Onboarding. SDK integration belongs in Platforms & SDKs. Test tooling, telemetry setup, provisioning, and release procedures have their own sections.

