# Feature Flags & Remote Configuration

Feature flags select between supported application behaviors without requiring a new binary for every configuration change. Remote configuration supplies those values through an approved service.

A flag can enable a feature already present in the installed app. It cannot add missing native code, replace server authorization, or guarantee an immediate change on an offline device.

**Outcome:** a feature behaves predictably with valid, missing, malformed, stale, or unavailable configuration, and its rollout can be verified and reversed through an owned process.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep real flag inventories, targeting rules, service configuration, and rollout evidence in approved systems. Remote configuration is client-accessible data, not a secret store. Examples below are illustrative contracts, not internal SDK APIs.

## 1. Choose the right kind of control

| Control | Purpose | Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Release flag** | Expose an implemented feature to an approved audience. | Enable the redesigned balance screen. |
| **Operational flag** | Disable an optional path or reduce dependency load. | Stop optional background refresh. |
| **Experiment assignment** | Compare supported variants with stable assignment and agreed measurement. | Compare two approved onboarding layouts. |
| **Configuration value** | Supply a bounded parameter. | A refresh interval within an approved range. |
| **Server authorization** | Decide whether an operation or resource is permitted. | Check that an authenticated user may access an account. |

Authorization belongs on the server even when a flag hides the corresponding UI. Never deliver credentials, signing material, privileged API keys, or confidential business rules as supposedly hidden configuration.

## 2. Define a flag contract before integration

For each flag, document:

| Field | Required decision |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Name and meaning** | What does true/false or each variant actually select? |
| **Owner** | Who approves changes, monitors rollout, and removes the flag? |
| **Local default** | What happens before any usable remote snapshot exists? |
| **Supported app versions** | Which installed versions understand the value and behavior? |
| **Validation** | Expected type, allowed values, ranges, and compatible schema. |
| **Scope** | Environment, app, platform, tenant, account, or experiment as applicable. |
| **Freshness** | Cache lifetime and whether last-known-good values remain usable when stale. |
| **Activation** | When fetched values may affect an active journey. |
| **Recovery** | How to roll back and what to do when refresh is unavailable. |
| **Removal** | When the temporary flag and obsolete implementation can be deleted. |

Defaults are feature-specific. Disabling a new optional screen may be safe; disabling a required security control is not. Do not define a universal rule that all flags default to false without considering their meaning.

## 3. Separate fetch, validation, activation, and evaluation

<MermaidZoom title="Configuration lifecycle: usable defaults before approved remote activation">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant APP as Application Startup
    participant STORE as Configuration Owner
    participant CACHE as Scoped Cache
    participant REMOTE as Approved Config Service
    participant FEATURE as Feature

    APP->>STORE: Initialize typed local defaults
    STORE->>CACHE: Read snapshot for current scope
    CACHE-->>STORE: Return cached snapshot or none
    STORE->>STORE: Validate scope, schema, and freshness
    FEATURE->>STORE: Read active feature decision
    STORE-->>FEATURE: Return usable current decision
    STORE->>REMOTE: Refresh through approved adapter
    alt Valid response for current scope
        REMOTE-->>STORE: Return candidate snapshot
        STORE->>STORE: Validate and activate at a safe boundary
        STORE->>CACHE: Persist permitted validated snapshot
    else Unavailable or invalid response
        REMOTE-->>STORE: Return failure or invalid candidate
        STORE->>STORE: Keep permitted current state or use defaults
    end
```

</MermaidZoom>

Fetching does not automatically mean activation. A change during checkout, form submission, or another stateful journey may need to wait until that journey ends. Define the activation boundary rather than allowing a background refresh to switch behavior unexpectedly.

Do not block every startup indefinitely on a configuration fetch. If a feature requires fresh configuration, bound the wait and define a safe unavailable state.

## 4. Centralize the configuration owner

| Responsibility | Owner |
| :--- | :--- |
| Fetching, authentication integration, and cancellation | Approved service adapter and configuration coordinator. |
| Schema/type validation | Boundary decoder or mapper. |
| Scope, version compatibility, cache age, and activation | Configuration owner. |
| Resolving a feature decision | Typed evaluator using the active snapshot. |
| Rendering or starting the selected path | Feature state owner and UI. |
| Permission enforcement | Backend authorization. |

Screens should consume typed decisions instead of fetching arbitrary keys independently. Use one established configuration lifecycle rather than a new fetch loop in each screen.

A practical decision can carry internal provenance: default, cache, or fresh remote value, together with its revision and scope generation. Keep diagnostic metadata separate from sensitive targeting attributes.

## 5. Integrate Unleash with the correct mobile SDK

The shared package link, [`unleash-client`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/unleash-client), is the **Node.js backend SDK**. It evaluates server-side configuration and uses backend credentials. Do not install it into a React Native application or place a backend token in a mobile binary. See the [official Node SDK reference](https://docs.getunleash.io/sdks/node).

Mobile clients use the Frontend API or Unleash Edge with a **frontend token**. Evaluation occurs at that service boundary; the mobile SDK consumes evaluated flags for its context. See the [SDK architecture overview](https://docs.getunleash.io/sdks).

| Platform | Mobile integration | Setup requirement |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Android | `io.getunleash:unleash-android` | Pin a reviewed version in the project's dependency catalog. |
| iOS | `Unleash/unleash-ios-sdk`, module `UnleashProxyClientSwift` | Pin a reviewed SPM release and commit resolution according to project policy. |
| React Native | `@unleash/unleash-react-native-sdk` with `unleash-proxy-client` | Confirm compatibility before adopting the dedicated SDK. Current upstream documentation specifies React Native 0.81+ and React 18+. |

A frontend token is client-distributed configuration, not a privileged backend secret. Scope it to the approved project/environment and obtain it through the platform owner. Do not create tokens or change provider permissions without the required access.

### Before writing code

Confirm the endpoint ending in `/api/frontend`, frontend token binding, app name, supported SDK version, environment/project scope, allowed context fields, and rollout owner. Define `balance_redesign` in the intended environment through the approved configuration process. It is an illustrative flag name, not an assertion that this flag exists internally.

**Attach screenshot — Unleash flag configuration and environment selection, with tokens and targeting identifiers redacted.**

The minimal integrations below demonstrate **non-sensitive, application-wide configuration without account targeting**. They do not implement authenticated cache isolation. Account-targeted use must satisfy Section 6 before it is enabled.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

### A. Add the reviewed dependency

**File: application module `build.gradle.kts`**

```kotlin
dependencies {
    // Define this version in gradle.properties or your approved version catalog.
    val unleashVersion = providers.gradleProperty("unleashVersion").get()
    implementation("io.getunleash:unleash-android:$unleashVersion")
}
```

**File: `src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`**, directly inside the manifest element:

```xml
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
```

### B. Construct once and start from composition

**File: `configuration/UnleashFactory.kt`**

```kotlin
import android.content.Context
import io.getunleash.android.DefaultUnleash
import io.getunleash.android.UnleashConfig

fun createUnleash(
    context: Context,
    frontendUrl: String,
    frontendToken: String,
    appName: String,
): DefaultUnleash {
    require(frontendUrl.startsWith("https://"))
    require(frontendToken.isNotBlank() && appName.isNotBlank())
    return DefaultUnleash(
        androidContext = context.applicationContext,
        unleashConfig = UnleashConfig.newBuilder(appName = appName)
            .proxyUrl(frontendUrl)
            .clientKey(frontendToken)
            .build(),
    )
}
```

Supply approved non-secret app configuration at the application composition boundary, retain the resulting instance, and call `start()` once there. Do not construct a client on each screen or poll readiness with `Thread.sleep`.

### C. Evaluate at a deliberate journey boundary

**File: `configuration/BalanceFeaturePolicy.kt`**

```kotlin
import io.getunleash.android.Unleash

fun selectBalanceRedesign(
    client: Unleash,
    appSupportsRedesign: Boolean,
    accessAllowed: Boolean,
): Boolean = appSupportsRedesign && accessAllowed &&
    client.isEnabled("balance_redesign")
```

Call from the feature state owner when starting the journey and retain that decision for the journey. A later visit can evaluate updated configuration. If the feature needs live changes, use the SDK's state listener and lifecycle-owned collection rather than assuming `isEnabled` observes updates itself.

Unknown flags are disabled by default. Initial reads can precede a remote response; do not interpret false as proof of a successful fetch. Retain one application-scoped client to avoid cache contention. See the [Android SDK setup and events](https://docs.getunleash.io/sdks/android) and [official sample imports](https://github.com/Unleash/unleash-android-sdk/blob/main/app/src/main/java/io/getunleash/unleashandroid/TestApplication.kt).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

### A. Add the reviewed Swift package

In Xcode, add `https://github.com/Unleash/unleash-ios-sdk.git` using the approved release constraint. Import `UnleashProxyClientSwift`. Do not copy the upstream placeholder `0.0.0` as a production version.

### B. Construct the client at the application boundary

**File: `Configuration/UnleashFactory.swift`**

```swift
import UnleashProxyClientSwift

@MainActor
func createUnleash(
    frontendURL: String,
    frontendToken: String,
    appName: String
) -> UnleashClient {
    precondition(frontendURL.hasPrefix("https://"))
    precondition(!frontendToken.isEmpty && !appName.isEmpty)
    return UnleashClient(
        unleashUrl: frontendURL,
        clientKey: frontendToken,
        appName: appName
    )
}
```

Retain the returned client in application composition and invoke `start()` once according to the selected SDK release's API. Do not start a new client from each SwiftUI view initializer.

### C. Read the decision for a new journey

**File: `Configuration/BalanceFeaturePolicy.swift`**

```swift
import UnleashProxyClientSwift

@MainActor
func selectBalanceRedesign(
    client: UnleashClient,
    appSupportsRedesign: Bool,
    accessAllowed: Bool
) -> Bool {
    appSupportsRedesign && accessAllowed &&
        client.isEnabled(name: "balance_redesign")
}
```

The state owner captures this decision when beginning the journey. If configuration arrives later, use it for the next journey or implement an explicit safe activation boundary. Do not imply that this synchronous read subscribes to updates.

For live configuration, connect the documented `ready`/`update` events to the app's state owner, marshal UI mutation to its actor, and manage subscription ownership. Validate callback behavior against the pinned release. See the [iOS SDK reference](https://docs.getunleash.io/sdks/ios).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

### A. Add compatible packages through the repository workflow

Use the project package manager and reviewed exact versions, then commit the lockfile. The package names are:

```text
@unleash/unleash-react-native-sdk
unleash-proxy-client
```

Follow their peer-dependency and native setup requirements. Do not install the backend `unleash-client` package as a substitute. Older React Native apps require a separate compatibility review; do not upgrade the framework solely to copy this example.

### B. Provide one stable configuration

**File: `configuration/FeatureFlagProvider.tsx`**

```tsx
import React, { type ComponentProps, type ReactNode } from 'react';
import { FlagProvider } from '@unleash/unleash-react-native-sdk';

type Config = NonNullable<ComponentProps<typeof FlagProvider>['config']>;

type Props = Readonly<{
  config: Config;
  children: ReactNode;
}>;

export function FeatureFlagProvider({ config, children }: Props) {
  return <FlagProvider config={config}>{children}</FlagProvider>;
}
```

Construct `config` once in application composition using approved `url`, `clientKey`, and `appName` values, then mount the provider around the part of the app consuming flags. Keep the object stable across renders. It is ordinary client configuration, not a backend credential binding.

### C. Consume the flag without duplicating it in Redux

**File: `features/balance/BalanceExperience.tsx`**

```tsx
import React, { type ReactNode } from 'react';
import { useFlag } from '@unleash/unleash-react-native-sdk';

type Props = Readonly<{
  appSupportsRedesign: boolean;
  accessAllowed: boolean;
  redesigned: ReactNode;
  legacy: ReactNode;
}>;

export function BalanceExperience(props: Props) {
  const enabled = useFlag('balance_redesign');
  const showNew = enabled && props.appSupportsRedesign && props.accessAllowed;
  return <>{showNew ? props.redesigned : props.legacy}</>;
}
```

This example intentionally reacts to SDK updates and is suitable only where switching presentation is safe. Freeze a decision in the feature state owner for a stateful journey that must not change midway.

Keep Redux Toolkit for the established application/session state. Consume the SDK flag directly rather than maintaining a second polling loop and mirrored flag store. Provider-owned remote state and Redux-owned business state have different owners.

The hook can return false before flag data arrives and may later use cached values. `flagsReady` is not proof of perpetual freshness. Avoid blocking the whole application indefinitely on readiness. The dedicated SDK uses AsyncStorage-backed caching; it is not encrypted secret storage. See the [React Native SDK guide](https://docs.getunleash.io/sdks/react-native).

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

### Boundary to preserve

The frontend service evaluates the targeting context; the application adds its supported-capability and permission checks. Backend authorization still applies. The configuration examples are minimal SDK integration, not a complete authenticated configuration coordinator or a claim of a verified company deployment.


## 6. Handle caching and account transitions

**Do not enable account targeting with the minimal examples unchanged.** SDK cache and context-update behavior must be reviewed for the pinned release. Hide account-targeted features while changing context, serialize changes, reject obsolete completions, and verify the refreshed scope before exposing the new decision. Use the provider’s supported cache partition/clear mechanisms where required; merely remounting a provider or changing a cache prefix does not establish complete isolation.


| Event | Required behavior |
| :--- | :--- |
| First launch with no cache | Use explicit defaults or the feature's defined unavailable state. |
| Valid matching cache | Use only under the agreed age and compatibility policy. |
| Expired cache | Apply the documented stale policy; never assume indefinite reuse. |
| Malformed remote response | Reject according to the candidate policy and preserve a permitted prior state or defaults. |
| Account or tenant switch | Invalidate old targeting context and prevent late responses from activating for the new scope. |
| Logout | Clear or replace account-specific state and prevent stale in-flight work from restoring it. |
| App upgrade | Revalidate stored schema and feature compatibility before reuse. |

Tie refresh results to a scope generation or equivalent ownership token. Cancellation helps but is not enough: a result that completes late must be rejected if its scope is obsolete.

Do not persist sensitive targeting attributes unless approved. A cache key is not an authorization boundary. Use a monotonic clock for in-process elapsed intervals where appropriate; persisted age across restarts requires an explicitly designed timestamp policy.

## 7. Roll out and roll back deliberately

1. Confirm that each targeted app version contains both the intended behavior and a safe alternative.
2. Verify disabled, enabled, missing, malformed, and stale scenarios locally.
3. Publish through the approved configuration change process with a recorded revision and owner.
4. Start with the approved test audience and verify the actual evaluated decision.
5. Expand exposure according to the agreed rollout plan and monitor defined signals.
6. Roll back when the agreed criteria are met, then verify client behavior and propagation.
7. Remove temporary flags and obsolete paths after the supported-version and rollback needs are resolved.

Do not invent percentage steps or universal performance thresholds. For percentage rollout or experiments, use the approved provider's stable assignment and scope rules. Do not randomize a user's variant on each render.

A UI rollback may not undo writes, migrations, or side effects already performed. Those changes need a compatible recovery design beyond the flag.

## 8. Understand kill-switch limits

A remote switch takes effect only after the app obtains and activates the new configuration. Offline clients, throttled fetches, cache policies, background restrictions, and installed app capabilities affect response time.

For high-risk operations, use server-side enforcement where required. A remote client flag alone is insufficient to guarantee immediate shutdown.

| Risk | Design response |
| :--- | :--- |
| Client cannot refresh | Safe local behavior and a documented freshness policy. |
| Existing journey is in progress | Defined interruption or completion semantics. |
| Old app lacks the switch | Version-aware rollout and another control where required. |
| A write already occurred | Reconciliation or business recovery, not merely hiding the UI. |
| Remote config service fails | Bounded fetch and explicitly permitted fallback. |

## 9. Test the complete lifecycle

| Test case | Expected result |
| :--- | :--- |
| Default snapshot | Optional redesign remains disabled. |
| Valid true plus supported capability and allowed access | Redesign is selected. |
| Any eligibility input is false | Redesign is not selected. |
| String, numeric, missing, or null boolean | Boundary policy rejects or falls back without coercion. |
| Valid remote false | False remains a legitimate value, not a missing-value signal. |
| Fresh matching cache | Decision follows the approved cache policy. |
| Expired or wrong-scope cache | Values are not reused outside policy. |
| Fetch fails or times out | Feature remains usable under its defined fallback. |
| Old-account fetch finishes after switch | Snapshot is not activated or persisted for the new account. |
| Refresh during a stateful journey | Activation follows the defined safe boundary. |
| Rollback | Both evaluated decision and observable behavior match the intended revision. |
| Logout and restart | Account-specific values do not leak across sessions. |

Test policy functions with plain values. Test the coordinator with a controlled service, cache, and clock. Test both feature paths at the UI level, then verify provider integration and target-device behavior separately.

Meet the defined 100% coverage requirement without excluding an inconvenient flag branch. A passing decision-function test does not prove fetch, cache, targeting, or activation correctness.

## 10. Observe decisions safely

Use approved diagnostics to distinguish configuration fetch success, validation rejection, fallback, activation, and feature exposure. Attach the permitted configuration revision and app release when useful.

An exposure event should mean the user actually encountered the selected behavior, not simply that the app downloaded a true flag. Avoid duplicate exposure events on every render.

Do not log complete payloads, targeting profiles, credentials, or sensitive identifiers. Observability defines the actual Datadog attributes and reporting conventions; this guide does not invent them.

### SDK integration acceptance checks

Before publication as a project-specific integration, run the pinned SDK on the assigned app:

1. Start without usable remote configuration and verify the intended default.
2. Fetch the disabled flag, then enable it in the approved test environment and observe the documented activation behavior.
3. Simulate unavailable service and inspect actual cache/readiness behavior without exposing credentials.
4. Restart the app and verify the persisted-state policy.
5. Test account transitions before adding any account-targeted flags.
6. Confirm rollback on each supported platform and record the SDK version and configuration revision.

**Attach screenshot — disabled and enabled feature behavior with the corresponding sanitized configuration evidence.**

The SDK calls were checked against official documentation. SDK packages were not installed, platform integrations were not built, and no live Unleash environment was contacted. Company endpoint, version approval, and authenticated cache policy remain project-specific requirements.

## 11. Integration checklist

- [ ] Flag meaning, owner, local default, scope, and removal plan are documented.
- [ ] Actual provider/SDK version and configuration contract are confirmed.
- [ ] Values are decoded and validated before activation.
- [ ] Missing and invalid values are distinct from valid false or zero values.
- [ ] Cache freshness and account/session invalidation are explicit.
- [ ] Old refresh results cannot activate after a scope change.
- [ ] Activation cannot unexpectedly corrupt an in-progress journey.
- [ ] Server authorization and privileged secrets remain separate from flags.
- [ ] Both paths, fallback, rollback, and propagation limits have evidence.
- [ ] Diagnostic events contain only permitted data.

